                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="org_4">Canterbury, Kent. Benedictine
                     cathedral priory of Holy Trinity or Christ Church</orgName> <p>"sx" (fol. 1r).
                     "D[istinctio] ijᵃ G[radus] vᵘˢ" (fol. ir). <note
                     type="MLGB3_medievalCatalogueNotes">cat. 48</note> <q type="pressmark">sx;
                     D.ii. G.v</q></p> <p>See also Barker-Benfield, <title>St Augustine’s Abbey,
                     Canterbury</title> (2008), p. 1800: “Belonged to ChCh for most of the Middle
                     Ages, with the 12th-cent. letter-mark (‘.S.X.’ top right margin of fol. 1r),
                     and on fol. ir (flyleaf, top centre) the later ChCh shelfmark ‘D. ijᵃ. G. vᵘˢ’;
                     prob. =no. 48 in Prior Eastry’s catalogue (<title>ALCD</title>, p.
                     21).”</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_122">Canterbury, Kent.
                     Benedictine abbey of St Augustine (originally of St Peter and St
                     Paul)</orgName><p>Later belonged to St Augustine's, Canterbury: <q>De librario
                     sancti Augustini extra Cantuariam</q> (fol. 1r, s. xv). Cf.
                     <title>ALCD</title>, no. 48 (pp. 21 and 506). This is not to be equated with
                     BA1.1431.</p> <p>See also Barker-Benfield, <title>St Augustine’s Abbey,
                     Canterbury</title> (2008), pp. 1800-1801: “Below the s/m is a 12th-cent. title,
                     presumably of ChCh, and below that is ‘De librario Sancti Augustini Extra
                     Cantuariam’, in a hand prob. s. xv2 … no other signs of StA ownership. ...
                     Wrongly identified with BA1.1431 by <title>ALCD</title> p. 521 (different
                     sec.-fol. readings).”</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">William Darrell in 1565.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Given by Sir Walter Cope (?1553–1614) in
